    "content": "Poop on a cruise ship is sent to advanced onboard wastewater treatment plants, which process both black water (toilets) and gray water (sinks/showers) using bacteria, filtration, and UV disinfection. Once treated to high standards, the purified water is discharged into the ocean far from land, while solid waste (sludge) is either incinerated or offloaded at port facilities. What Happens to Waste on a Cruise Ship: Vacuum Systems: Toilets use vacuum suction to transport waste, reducing water usage. Onboard Treatment: Modern vessels act like floating municipalities, utilizing biological treatment and bioreactors to break down waste. Discharge Regulations: Treated wastewater is discharged at sea, adhering to international regulations (MARPOL) which often require being at least 3-12 nautical miles from land. Solid Waste (Sludge): Solid remnants (microbe sludge) are incinerated on board or offloaded at port facilities for land-based disposal. Water Quality: Treated effluent often meets high standards, sometimes cleaner than standard municipal sewage treatment, but it is rarely, if ever, reused for drinking. While some reports indicate that older ships or specific situations might allow for direct discharge, the industry standard has shifted toward rigorous on-board treatment.",
